## Overview

`SearchMenu` is a reusable command-palette component built on shadcn's `Command` primitive. Pass it grouped items and it renders a responsive search trigger, `⌘K`/`Ctrl+K` shortcut handling, searchable groups, optional item icons, descriptions, and shortcuts. Labels are stored in the component file and can be overridden with props or the `messages` prop.

## Features

- Generic grouped item input
- Optional item icons, descriptions, keywords, disabled state, and shortcuts
- Per-item `onSelect` handlers plus a top-level `onSelect` callback
- Controlled or uncontrolled open state
- Mobile icon-only trigger and desktop search-bar trigger

## Usage

```tsx
import { SearchMenu, type SearchMenuGroup } from "@/components/ui/search-menu";
import { FileText, Settings } from "lucide-react";

const groups: SearchMenuGroup[] = [
  {
    heading: "Pages",
    items: [
      {
        id: "docs",
        label: "Docs",
        description: "Open documentation",
        icon: <FileText className="size-4" />,
        onSelect: () => navigate({ to: "/docs" }),
      },
    ],
  },
  {
    heading: "Settings",
    items: [
      {
        id: "profile",
        label: "Profile",
        icon: <Settings className="size-4" />,
      },
    ],
  },
];

<SearchMenu
  groups={groups}
  messages={{ title: "Search", description: "Search pages and actions." }}
/>
```

## Overview

`IconInput` is a controlled icon picker for any [Iconify](https://icon-sets.iconify.design/) collection. It uses `VirtualizedCombobox` to search and render large result sets efficiently, and returns the selected icon name without its collection prefix. Search results are loaded through an Effect Atom and `HttpClient`.

## Usage

```tsx
import { useState } from "react";
import { IconInput } from "@/components/ui/icon-input";

const [icon, setIcon] = useState("circle-help");

<IconInput value={icon} onValueChange={setIcon} />
```

Pass `collection` to use another Iconify collection.

## Overview

`Pagination` is a controlled paginator for lists, cards, and tables. It includes a page-size selector, current-page status, first/previous/next/last navigation, optional result and selection summaries, and a compact mode for narrow containers. English and French labels are built in and can be overridden through `messages`.

## Usage

```tsx
import { Pagination } from "@/components/ui/pagination";

<Pagination
  page={page}
  pageCount={pageCount}
  pageSize={pageSize}
  totalRows={total}
  onPageChange={setPage}
  onPageSizeChange={(size) => {
    setPage(0);
    setPageSize(size);
  }}
/>
```

Use `compact` in sidebars or narrow result panels, and `showResults={false}` when the surrounding view already displays a result count.

## Overview

`DataTable` uses decoded `QueryType` as its complete public state. Pagination, filtering, and structured sorting can be shared directly by TanStack Router, an Effect Atom request, and the table without `Schema.encodeSync`. Table-only state stays internal. Column visibility, column sizing, and view persist with `Atom.kvs` and browser local storage; selection, grouping, and collapsed groups reset on mount. The component derives its storage identity from the current pathname, normalized column IDs, and whether gallery view is available.

## Usage

```tsx
<DataTable
  rowData={assets}
  columnDefs={[
    { field: "name", headerName: "Name" },
    {
      field: "status",
      headerName: "Status",
      cellRenderer: ({ value }) => <Badge>{value}</Badge>,
    },
  ]}
  getRowId={(row) => row.id}
  features={{
    search: true,
    pagination: { mode: "client" },
    selection: { onSelectionChange: setSelectedAssets },
  }}
/>
```

Define `columnDefs` with `field` and `headerName`; use `cellRenderer` for custom cells. Supply `getRowId` whenever rows have stable identifiers. Configure search, sorting, client or server pagination, column visibility, selection and bulk actions, row actions, export, refresh, grouping, gallery view, and reordering through `features`.

## Router And Atom

Validate the route with `QueryStandard`. The decoded search object can be passed unchanged to an Atom-backed request and `DataTable`. Query changes navigate directly with no encoding step. UI changes remain internal and do not trigger `onStateChange` or URL updates.

```tsx
import { useAtomValue } from "@effect/atom-react";
import { AsyncResult, Atom } from "effect/unstable/reactivity";
import { createFileRoute } from "@tanstack/react-router";

import { DataTable } from "@/components/ui/data-table";
import { QueryStandard, type QueryType } from "@/lib/query";
import { ApiKeys } from "@/services/api-keys";

const apiKeysAtom = Atom.family((query: QueryType) =>
  Atom.make(ApiKeys.list({ query })),
);

export const Route = createFileRoute("/admin/api-keys")({
  validateSearch: QueryStandard,
  component: ApiKeysPage,
});

function ApiKeysPage() {
  const state = Route.useSearch();
  const navigate = Route.useNavigate();
  const result = useAtomValue(apiKeysAtom(state));
  const rows = AsyncResult.match(result, {
    onInitial: () => [],
    onFailure: () => [],
    onSuccess: ({ value }) => value,
  });

  return (
    <DataTable
      columnDefs={columns}
      rowData={rows}
      state={state}
      onStateChange={(next) =>
        void navigate({ replace: true, search: next })
      }
    />
  );
}
```

Use `initialState` instead when route search only supplies mount-time defaults. Tables on different routes, with different column identities, or with different view capabilities persist independently.

## Interactions

Selection supports stable IDs, row eligibility, change callbacks, and bulk actions. Grouping supports nested fields, empty groups, group actions, and moving rows between groups; gallery and row reordering use the same row model. Reorderable rows include Move up and Move down actions by default. Column resizing, row reordering, and group moves use integrated Pointer Events with pointer capture, so no drag-and-drop dependency is needed.

## Overview

`FilePicker` is a form-library-independent controlled file input. It combines a visually hidden native input with an accessible choose button, drag-and-drop selection, shadcn Attachment presentation, file metadata, image previews, and replace/delete actions. English and French labels are built in and can be overridden through `messages`.

## Usage

```tsx
const [file, setFile] = useState<File>();

<FilePicker
  accept="application/pdf"
  canClear={Boolean(file)}
  file={file}
  id="document"
  name="document"
  onChange={setFile}
  onClear={() => setFile(undefined)}
  title={file?.name ?? "Document"}
/>
```

Pass `image={{ alt, width, height, src }}` to enable image previews. `src` can represent a persisted image while a newly selected `file` is previewed through a managed object URL.

## Overview

`theme-switcher` provides a compact dropdown for switching between light, dark, and system themes. It persists the selected theme in `localStorage`, applies the resolved theme class to the document element, and renders a `ScriptOnce` startup script from `ThemeProvider` to avoid theme flash. Labels are stored in the component file and can be overridden with the `messages` prop.

## Usage

Wrap your app in `ThemeProvider`.

```tsx
import { ThemeProvider } from "@/components/ui/theme-switcher";

<ThemeProvider>{children}</ThemeProvider>
```

Render the switcher from a component inside the provider.

```tsx
import { ThemeSwitcher, useTheme } from "@/components/ui/theme-switcher";

const { theme, setTheme } = useTheme();

<ThemeSwitcher value={theme} onChange={setTheme} />

<ThemeSwitcher
  value={theme}
  onChange={setTheme}
  messages={{ title: "Theme", light: "Day", dark: "Night" }}
/>
```

## Overview

`notification-menu` provides backend-neutral notification presentation and interactions. It can present inbox records from the recommended `@krak-stack/notifications` package or from an application-owned compatibility store. The component itself never fetches, persists, or authorizes records.

The consumer owns trusted user and tenant scope, HTTP and atom integration, mutations, invalidation, navigation, localized notification content, and branding. The component receives already-authorized notifications and reports IDs to `onMarkRead` and `onArchive`. English and French menu chrome is built in and can be overridden with `messages`.

## Usage

```tsx
import { NotificationMenu } from "@/services/notification/client";

<NotificationMenu
  notifications={notifications}
  onMarkRead={(notificationIds) => client.markRead({ notificationIds })}
  onArchive={(notificationIds) => client.archive({ notificationIds })}
  onNavigate={(notification) => navigate({ href: notification.href })}
/>
```

Map durable package inbox records to `NotificationItem` only after the server has enforced recipient, organization, and workspace scope. Each item accepts `id`, `title`, optional `description`, `createdAt`, `timestampLabel`, `href`, `readAt`, and `archivedAt`. Opening the menu marks unread inbox IDs as read by default; set `markReadOnOpen={false}` to disable this behavior.

Use `renderItem` for custom item content and `renderTrigger` for custom trigger presentation. Never pass browser-supplied scope directly to backend list or mutation operations.

## Compatibility Status

`notification-channel-email-ses` is the SES adapter for the source-vendored `service-notification` direct dispatcher. It handles that dispatcher's top-level `email` message key and keeps its compatibility payload schema beside the channel.

New durable systems should install `@krak-stack/notifications` and import `sesEmailTransportRegistryLayer` from `@krak-stack/notifications/transport/email-ses`. That package adapter participates in the delivery ledger, attempts, preferences and suppressions, retries, scheduler, and workers.

## Included Files

- `services/notification/channels/ses/index.ts` - Compatibility SES channel, config service, and layer
- `services/notification/channels/ses/schema.ts` - Compatibility SES payload schema

## Direct Compatibility Usage

```ts
const notificationChannelsLayer = Layer.effect(
  NotificationChannelRegistry,
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const email = yield* SesNotificationChannel;
    return NotificationChannelRegistry.make(email);
  }),
).pipe(Layer.provide(SesNotificationChannel.layer));

const notificationServiceLayer = NotificationService.layer.pipe(
  Layer.provide(notificationChannelsLayer),
);
```

Use this path only when the application intentionally keeps the legacy direct dispatcher. Direct delivery runs in the calling process and has no package-owned persistence, retries, preferences, or worker runtime.

## Environment Variables

- `SES_REGION` - AWS SES region
- `SES_ACCESS_KEY_ID` - AWS access key ID
- `SES_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` - AWS secret access key
- `NOTIFICATION_EMAIL_FROM` - Optional default sender address when the payload omits `from`

## Overview

`service-health` provides a configurable `HealthService`, documented Effect schemas, a `HealthApiGroup`, and an exported handler implementation. It exposes `/health`, `/health/live`, `/health/ready`, and `/health/started`. Successful checks return `UP`; any failed check produces `DOWN`, and the HTTP contract maps unhealthy responses to status 503. Package consumers import the complete public API from `@krak-stack/registry/service-health`.

## Included Files

- `services/health/index.ts` - public barrel and health service
- `services/health/schema.ts` - health response schemas and HTTP status metadata
- `services/health/api.group.ts` - public health API group
- `services/health/api.builder.ts` - reusable handler implementation

## Register The API Group

Add `HealthApiGroup` after applying authentication middleware to application groups so health endpoints remain public. Apply the root prefix after adding the health group so every endpoint receives the same prefix.

```ts
import { HealthApiGroup } from "@/services/health";

export const Api = HttpApi.make("AppApi")
  .add(ApplicationApiGroup)
  .middleware(AuthMiddleware)
  .add(HealthApiGroup)
  .prefix("/api");
```

## Build And Register The Handler

Bind the exported `healthHandler` implementation to the parent API group. Configure checks with `HealthService.layerWith`, then provide the resulting layer to the application's `HttpApiBuilder` layer. The handler automatically uses the paths and prefix already present on `Api`. Check dependencies such as `DB` are captured when the health service layer is built.

```ts
import { Effect, Layer } from "effect";
import { HttpApiBuilder } from "effect/unstable/httpapi";

import { Api } from "@/api";
import { DB } from "@/services/database";
import { healthHandler, HealthService } from "@/services/health";

const healthLayer = HttpApiBuilder.group(Api, "health", healthHandler).pipe(
  Layer.provide(
    HealthService.layerWith({
      checks: {
        ready: [
          {
            name: "database",
            check: Effect.gen(function* () {
              const db = yield* DB;
              yield* db.$client`SELECT 1`;
              return HealthService.up();
            }).pipe(Effect.timeout("2 seconds")),
          },
        ],
      },
    }),
  ),
);

export const apiLayer = HttpApiBuilder.layer(Api).pipe(
  Layer.provide(healthLayer),
  Layer.provide(DB.layer),
);
```

Failed effects, defects, and timeouts become `DOWN` results without exposing internal errors. Optional diagnostic data accepts non-sensitive string, number, and boolean values.

## Overview

`service-s3` provides an Effect service around Bun's built-in S3 client. It reads S3 configuration from environment variables, exposes a reusable `S3Service.layer`, and maps client failures into `S3ServiceError` values.

## Included Files

- `services/s3/index.ts` - S3 config service, S3 client service, production layer, and test layer helper
- `services/s3/schema.ts` - Typed `S3ServiceError` schema

## Usage

```ts
import { Effect } from "effect";
import { S3Service } from "@/services/s3";

const program = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const s3 = yield* S3Service;

  yield* s3.write("uploads/hello.txt", "Hello world", {
    type: "text/plain",
  });

  const text = yield* s3.text("uploads/hello.txt");
  const url = yield* s3.presign("uploads/hello.txt", { expiresIn: 60 });

  return { text, url };
});

const result = await Effect.runPromise(
  program.pipe(Effect.provide(S3Service.layer)),
);
```

## Available Operations

- `file(path, options?)` - Create a Bun `S3File` reference
- `write(path, data, options?)` - Write object data
- `text(path)`, `json(path)`, `bytes(path)`, `arrayBuffer(path)`, `stream(path)` - Read object data
- `exists(path)`, `stat(path)`, `size(path)` - Read object metadata
- `delete(path)` - Delete an object
- `presign(path, options?)` - Create a presigned object URL
- `list(options?)` - List bucket objects

## Environment Variables

- `S3_ACCESS_KEY_ID` - S3 access key ID
- `S3_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY` - S3 secret access key
- `S3_BUCKET` - S3 bucket name
- `S3_REGION` - Optional S3 region
- `S3_ENDPOINT` - Optional custom S3-compatible endpoint
- `S3_SESSION_TOKEN` - Optional session token

This service uses Bun's `S3Client`, so it is intended for Bun runtimes or compatible S3-compatible deployments.

## Overview

`service-cloudflare-ai-gateway` is a copy/paste Effect service for Cloudflare's OpenAI-compatible REST API. It reads `AI_GATEWAY_BASE_URL`, `AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY`, and `AI_GATEWAY_ID`, then applies bearer authorization and the required `cf-aig-gateway-id` header.

Use `CloudflareAIGateway.layer` when no custom metadata is needed. Use `CloudflareAIGateway.layerWith(metadata)` to attach up to five string, number, or boolean values through `cf-aig-metadata`. Use the `userId` metadata key for Cloudflare User Insights attribution.

## Complete Layer

```ts
import { OpenAiClient } from "@effect/ai-openai";
import { Config, Context, Effect, Layer } from "effect";
import {
  FetchHttpClient,
  HttpClient,
  HttpClientRequest,
} from "effect/unstable/http";

export type CloudflareAIGatewayMetadata = Readonly<
  Record<string, string | number | boolean>
>;

export class CloudflareAIGateway extends Context.Service<CloudflareAIGateway>()(
  "CloudflareAIGateway",
  {
    make: Effect.gen(function* () {
      const apiUrl = yield* Config.string("AI_GATEWAY_BASE_URL");
      const apiKey = yield* Config.redacted("AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY");
      const gatewayId = yield* Config.string("AI_GATEWAY_ID");
      return { apiUrl, apiKey, gatewayId };
    }),
  },
) {
  static readonly baseLayer = Layer.effect(this, this.make);

  private static readonly clientLayer = (
    metadata?: CloudflareAIGatewayMetadata,
  ) =>
    Layer.unwrap(
      Effect.map(this, ({ apiKey, apiUrl, gatewayId }) =>
        OpenAiClient.layer({
          apiKey,
          apiUrl,
          transformClient: (client) =>
            HttpClient.mapRequest(client, (request) => {
              const gatewayRequest = HttpClientRequest.setHeader(
                request,
                "cf-aig-gateway-id",
                gatewayId,
              );
              return metadata
                ? HttpClientRequest.setHeader(
                    gatewayRequest,
                    "cf-aig-metadata",
                    JSON.stringify(metadata),
                  )
                : gatewayRequest;
            }),
        }),
      ),
    ).pipe(
      Layer.provide(FetchHttpClient.layer),
      Layer.provide(this.baseLayer),
    );

  static readonly layer = this.clientLayer();
  static readonly layerWith = (metadata: CloudflareAIGatewayMetadata) =>
    this.clientLayer(metadata);
}
```

## Usage

```ts
import { OpenAiLanguageModel } from "@effect/ai-openai";
import { Layer } from "effect";
import { CloudflareAIGateway } from "@/services/cloudflare-ai-gateway";

const model = OpenAiLanguageModel.model("openai/gpt-5.6-luna", {
  reasoning: { effort: "low" },
}).pipe(
  Layer.provide(
    CloudflareAIGateway.layerWith({
      userId: "user-id",
      organizationId: "organization-id",
    }),
  ),
);
```

Third-party models must use Cloudflare's provider-qualified `provider/model` form, such as `openai/gpt-5.6-luna`. Workers AI models use the `@cf/author/model` form.

## Environment Variables

- `AI_GATEWAY_BASE_URL` - Cloudflare OpenAI-compatible REST API base URL
- `AI_GATEWAY_API_KEY` - Cloudflare API token with Workers AI Read permission
- `AI_GATEWAY_ID` - AI Gateway ID sent in `cf-aig-gateway-id`

## Overview

`service-file-extraction` extracts Markdown text from document bytes through Xberg. It provides bounded input and output sizes, UTF-8-safe truncation, concurrency control, extraction timeouts, archive security limits, and typed Effect failures.

## Example: Extract an S3 Document to Markdown

Read the object through `S3Service`, then pass its bytes and stored metadata to the extraction service. The returned `content` is Markdown. Install `@krak-stack/service-s3` separately when using this integration.

```ts
import { Effect, Layer } from "effect";
import { FileExtractionService } from "@/services/file-extraction";
import { S3Service } from "@/services/s3";

const objectToMarkdown = ({
  objectKey,
  filename,
  mimeType,
}: {
  objectKey: string;
  filename: string;
  mimeType: string;
}) =>
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const s3 = yield* S3Service;
    const extraction = yield* FileExtractionService;
    const bytes = yield* s3.bytes(objectKey);
    const result = yield* extraction.markdown({ bytes, filename, mimeType });
    return result.content;
  });

const layer = Layer.merge(S3Service.layer, FileExtractionService.layer);
const markdown = await Effect.runPromise(
  objectToMarkdown({
    objectKey: "uploads/document.pdf",
    filename: "document.pdf",
    mimeType: "application/pdf",
  }).pipe(Effect.provide(layer)),
);
```

## Example: Extract In-Memory Bytes

Use the extraction service directly when bytes have already been loaded from another source.

```ts
const result = await Effect.runPromise(
  Effect.gen(function* () {
    const extraction = yield* FileExtractionService;
    return yield* extraction.markdown({
      bytes,
      filename: "document.pdf",
      mimeType: "application/pdf",
    });
  }).pipe(Effect.provide(FileExtractionService.layer)),
);
```

## API

```ts
type FileExtractionInput = {
  bytes: Uint8Array;
  filename: string;
  mimeType: string;
};

type ExtractFileInput = FileExtractionInput & {
  outputFormat: OutputFormat;
};

type FileExtractedText = {
  content: string;
  contentByteSize: number;
  truncated: boolean;
};

extraction.markdown(input: FileExtractionInput)
extraction.text(input: FileExtractionInput)
extraction.html(input: FileExtractionInput)
extraction.extract(input: ExtractFileInput)
```

- `markdown` renders `OutputFormat.Markdown`.
- `text` renders `OutputFormat.Plain`.
- `html` renders `OutputFormat.Html`.
- `extract` requires any Xberg `OutputFormat`, including `Djot`, `Json`, `Structured`, and `Custom`.

## Output Formats

Use `markdown`, `text`, or `html` when the desired format is known. Use `extract` with a required Xberg `OutputFormat` for lower-level access to any supported format.

```ts
import { OutputFormat } from "@/services/file-extraction";

const markdown = yield* extraction.markdown(input);
const text = yield* extraction.text(input);
const html = yield* extraction.html(input);
const djot = yield* extraction.extract({
  ...input,
  outputFormat: OutputFormat.Djot,
});
```

All methods return the same bounded result shape with `content`, `contentByteSize`, and `truncated`.

## Configuration

The default layer accepts inputs up to 20 MB, returns up to 512 KB of Markdown, runs two extractions concurrently, and applies a 30-second timeout. Use `layerWith` to override operational limits while retaining secure extraction defaults.

```ts
const layer = FileExtractionService.layerWith({
  maxInputBytes: 10 * 1024 * 1024,
  maxOutputBytes: 256 * 1024,
  maxConcurrentExtractions: 4,
  timeoutSeconds: 20,
});
```

## Overview

`service-database` provides a Drizzle PostgreSQL client as an Effect service. It reads `DATABASE_URL`, configures pg type parsing for date and interval types, and exposes a reusable layer for application programs.

## Usage

```ts
import { Effect } from "effect";
import { DB } from "@/services/database";
import { users } from "@/db/schema";

const program = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const db = yield* DB;
  return yield* db.select().from(users);
});

const usersList = await Effect.runPromise(
  program.pipe(Effect.provide(DB.layer)),
);
```

## Overview

`workflow-layer` installs the shared `ClusterWorkflowEngine` layer used to run durable Effect workflows. It uses PostgreSQL for durable workflow state and a single in-memory runner suitable for a single application process.

Only `services/workflow.ts` is installed. The example below is documentation for adapting to an application-owned service; it is not copied into the receiving project.

## Shared Workflow Engine Layer

The installed shared layer is small enough to use directly:

```ts
import { Layer } from "effect";
import { ClusterWorkflowEngine, SingleRunner } from "effect/unstable/cluster";

import { DB } from "@/services/database";

export const workflowEngineLayer = ClusterWorkflowEngine.layer.pipe(
  Layer.provide(SingleRunner.layer({ runnerStorage: "memory" })),
  Layer.provide(DB.clientLayer),
);
```

## Durable Workflow Example

Define workflows beside the service they orchestrate, for example in `src/services/example/workflows.ts`. This versioned workflow uses a stable caller-provided idempotency key and wraps persistence in a durable activity.

```ts
import { Effect, Schema } from "effect";
import { Activity, Workflow } from "effect/unstable/workflow";

import { ExampleSchema } from "./schema";
import { Examples } from "./service";

export const CreateExampleV1 = Workflow.make("CreateExampleV1", {
  payload: {
    requestId: Schema.String,
    userId: Schema.String,
    name: Schema.NonEmptyString,
    description: Schema.optional(Schema.String),
  },
  success: ExampleSchema,
  idempotencyKey: ({ requestId }) => requestId,
});

export const examplesWorkflowLayer = CreateExampleV1.toLayer(
  ({ userId, name, description }) =>
    Effect.gen(function* () {
      const examples = yield* Examples;

      return yield* Activity.make({
        name: "PersistExampleV1",
        success: ExampleSchema,
        execute: examples.create({
          userId,
          payload: { name, description },
        }),
      });
    }),
);
```

`ExampleSchema` is the serializable result schema and `Examples` is the application-owned domain service. Keep HTTP and authentication concerns outside the workflow and pass validated actor identifiers in its payload.

## Application Setup

Provide each workflow implementation with its domain service, then provide the shared engine at the application composition root.

```ts
import { Layer } from "effect";

import { examplesWorkflowLayer } from "@/services/example/workflows";
import { Examples } from "@/services/example";
import { workflowEngineLayer } from "@/services/workflow";

export const workflowsLayer = examplesWorkflowLayer.pipe(
  Layer.provide(Examples.layer),
  Layer.provide(workflowEngineLayer),
);
```

Start `CreateExampleV1` through Effect's workflow APIs with a unique `requestId`. Reusing the same request ID preserves idempotency across retries.

## Production Considerations

`SingleRunner.layer({ runnerStorage: "memory" })` is intended for a single process. Replace the runner topology when deploying multiple workflow workers. Workflow and activity names are persisted compatibility boundaries, so version names rather than renaming an active workflow in place.

## Overview

`localization` provides request-scoped locale context for Effect HttpApi applications using Paraglide. It resolves the locale from the `locale` URL query parameter, the `locale` request header, the `locale` cookie, or `Accept-Language`, in that order, then provides that context to downstream handlers through `LocaleMiddleware`. All explicit locale values are validated with Effect Schema and cookies are parsed with Effect's HTTP cookie utilities. The `localize` helper selects a record translation using the current locale and fallback settings.

## Included Files

- `lib/localization.ts` - `LocaleContext`, `LocaleMiddleware`, `LocaleMiddlewareLive`, `LocalizedInputType`, and `localize`

## Requirements

The receiving project must use Paraglide and expose its generated runtime at `@/paraglide/runtime`. Supported locales are derived from the receiving project's Paraglide configuration.

## API Setup

Register the live middleware layer in the application HTTP API layer:

```ts
import { Layer } from "effect";
import { LocaleMiddlewareLive } from "@/lib/localization";

const apiLayer = Layer.mergeAll(/* application layers */).pipe(
  Layer.provide(LocaleMiddlewareLive),
);
```

Apply `LocaleMiddleware` to an API group and read `LocaleContext` in endpoint handlers:

```ts
import { Effect } from "effect";
import { HttpApiGroup } from "effect/unstable/httpapi";
import { LocaleContext, LocaleMiddleware } from "@/lib/localization";

const RecordsGroup = HttpApiGroup.make("records").middleware(LocaleMiddleware);

const program = Effect.gen(function* () {
  const { locale, fallbackLocale } = yield* LocaleContext;
  // Use locale and fallbackLocale to resolve localized data.
});
```

## Translation Resolution

`localize` expects a record with a `translations` array whose items have a `locale` field. It returns the matching translation merged with the base record, falls back to `fallbackLocale` or the first translation, and respects `fallbackLocale: "none"` when a translation must exactly match.

```ts
import { localize, type LocalizedInputType } from "@/lib/localization";

const context: LocalizedInputType = { locale: "fr", fallbackLocale: "en" };
const record = localize(context, {
  id: "welcome",
  translations: [
    { locale: "en", name: "Welcome" },
    { locale: "fr", name: "Bienvenue" },
  ],
});
```

## Overview

`query-helpers` provides reusable Effect query helpers for API endpoints that accept paginated, filtered, and sorted query input and return paginated lists. Use `Query` for request validation, `encodeSortParam` and `decodeSortParam` for table sort query strings, `PaginationMeta` for response metadata, and `PaginatedResponse` to wrap item schemas in a consistent `{ data, meta }` envelope.

## Query Input

`Query` validates the request query parameters accepted by list endpoints:

- `page` - zero-based page index. The first page is `0`.
- `pageSize` - number of records to request per page. Must be between `1` and `100`.
- `globalFilter` - optional free-text filter value for server-side search.
- `sort` - optional sort value encoded as `field:direction`, where direction is `asc` or `desc`.

Example query input:

```ts
{
  page: 0,
  pageSize: 10,
  globalFilter: "housing",
  sort: "publicName:asc",
}
```

## Sort Helpers

`encodeSortParam` converts the first TanStack Table-style sorting entry into a compact query string. `decodeSortParam` parses a sort query string back into `{ id, direction }` and returns `null` when the input is missing or invalid.

```ts
const sort = encodeSortParam([{ id: "publicName", desc: false }]);
// "publicName:asc"

const parsed = decodeSortParam(sort);
// { id: "publicName", direction: "asc" }
```

## Included Files

- `lib/query.ts` - `Query`, `QueryType`, `SortDirection`, `SortState`, `SortParam`, `encodeSortParam`, `decodeSortParam`, `PaginationMeta`, `PaginatedResponse`, and exported schema types

## Usage

```ts
import { Query, PaginatedResponse, decodeSortParam } from "@/lib/query";
import { Record } from "@/services/record/schema";

export const PaginatedRecords = PaginatedResponse(Record);

HttpApiEndpoint.get("listRecords", "/records", {
  query: Query,
  success: PaginatedRecords,
});

const sort = decodeSortParam(query.sort);
```

Return data with the matching envelope. `meta.page` and `meta.pageSize` should mirror the accepted query input, while `total` and `pageCount` describe the full result set:

```ts
return {
  data: rows,
  meta: {
    page,
    pageSize,
    total,
    pageCount: Math.ceil(total / pageSize),
  },
};
```

